**Master Template**

```sql
SELECT d.name, MAX(e.salary) AS max_salary
FROM Employee e
JOIN Department d ON e.departmentId = d.id
GROUP BY d.name;
```

**Basic Practice Problems**

- K1. `Employee(id, name, salary, department_id)`, `Department(id, name)` — average salary per department name (not id).
- K2. Highest salary per department, showing department name and employee name (tuple-match approach).
- K3. `Orders(order_id, customer_id, amount)`, `Customers(id, name)` — total amount spent per customer name, only customers spending > 1000.
- K4. `Project(project_id, employee_id)`, `Employee(employee_id, name, experience_years)` — average experience per project.
- K5. Departments with more than 3 employees, showing department name (JOIN + GROUP BY + HAVING combined).


answers
1.
select d.name , AVG(salary)
from employee e
join department d
on e.department_id =d.id
group by d.name;


2.
select d.name , e.name, MAX(e.salary) as high_sal
from employee e
join department d
on e.department_id =d.id
group by d.name;


3.- K3. `Orders(order_id, customer_id, amount)`, `Customers(id, name)` — total amount spent per customer name, only customers spending > 1000.

select c.name , SUM(o.amount) as total_amount
from customer c
join orders o
on o.customer_id=c.id

group by c.name
having SUM(o.amount) >1000;


